 # MSE Loss and PSNR Conversion
 
-Also called: Loss Function for Constant Model, MSE Loss, Mean Squared Error, Mean Squared Error Loss
+Also called: Loss Function for Constant Model, MSE Loss, Mean Squared Error, Mean Squared Error Loss, Pixel-wise RGB Mean Squared Error (MSE)
 
 ## Overview
-MSE Loss and PSNR Conversion computes the Mean Squared Error (MSE) between predicted and target RGB values (or volumes) and converts this error into Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) for logging and evaluation [S2, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It serves as the primary photometric consistency loss for training differentiable volume rendering models, minimizing the pixel-wise intensity difference between rendered images and ground truth [S4, unofficial][S7, unofficial]. Unlike structural metrics such as SSIM, which measure perceptual similarity, MSE focuses strictly on pixel-wise intensity accuracy, making it sensitive to large smooth regions while largely ignoring small, high-frequency features like specular highlights [S1, unofficial].
